      "Lowest-cost way to cut radiant heat from a hot door",
      "Light and quick to install",
      "Useful for sun-facing doors in hot climates"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No published R-value",
      "Little help against winter heat loss",
      "Owners report needing extra adhesive"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a sun-blasted door in summer, the Reach Barrier kit is a cheap, sensible fix; it is not the kit for a cold winter garage."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Garage Door Insulation Kits",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-garage-door-insulation-kit/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best garage door insulation kit?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For an uninsulated single steel door, the Owens Corning kit is the best all-round choice because Owens Corning publishes R-8 under ASTM C177, the highest figure among the panel kits we found. If you prefer rigid foam panels with a cleaner finish, the Matador, Cellofoam and Insulfoam kits publish roughly R-4.8 to R-5.2 per panel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is insulating a garage door worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually yes for attached or heated garages, and often for comfort in unheated ones. A bare steel door has almost no insulating value, so even a modest kit noticeably reduces heat flow through the door. Pair it with new seals around the door for the biggest difference."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What R-value do I need for a garage door?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is no single requirement, but higher is better in cold climates and heated garages. Among common kits, faced fiberglass at R-8 is the highest published figure we found, and EPS panel kits are around R-5. Reflective kits often publish no R-value or quote figures that depend on an air gap."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do reflective garage door insulation kits work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They work best against radiant heat, such as a steel door baking in afternoon sun, and can make a hot garage noticeably more comfortable. They are much less effective against winter heat loss. Be skeptical of high R-values quoted for thin reflective products."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will insulation make my garage door too heavy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most kits add only a few pounds, and reflective kits add very little. It can still shift the door's balance. Disconnect the opener and lift the door halfway; if it does not stay put, have a technician adjust the springs rather than doing it yourself."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I insulate a double garage door?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Most single-door panel kits are used two at a time on a 16- or 18-foot double door, and some reflective kits are sold in two-car sizes. Measure the door and the pans, since double doors often have wider pans than single doors."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you insulate a wood or glass garage door with a kit?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "These kits are designed for single-skin steel doors with open pans. Wood doors can trap moisture behind insulation, and glass panels cannot be insulated with a kit. If your door is wood or largely glass, a replacement insulated door is usually the better route."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
