          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Installing a Kit</a>
          <a href="#weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Weight, Balance and Springs</a>
          <a href="#sealing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Seal the Gaps Too</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cost and Payback</a>
          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip a Kit</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A bare steel garage door is little more than a sheet of metal between your garage and the weather, and an insulation kit is the cheapest way to change that. The best kit for most single steel doors is a rigid panel kit with a published R-value, such as Owens Corning's R-8 fiberglass kit or a Matador or Cellofoam polystyrene kit, because those numbers come with a test standard behind them. Reflective foil kits are cheaper and easier but publish weaker or no R-values, and they help most against summer sun rather than winter cold. Whatever you buy, seal the gaps around the door too, or the insulation will barely register.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garage door insulation kits are sold with some of the boldest claims in home improvement: temperature drops of 20 or 40 degrees, R-values that seem impossible for the thickness, and promises of lower heating bills for the whole house. So we started with the one number that has a test method behind it, the R-value, and asked a simple question of each kit: does the maker publish it, and under what conditions?
          </p>

          <p>
            Panel kits from insulation manufacturers generally do. Owens Corning lists R-8 under ASTM C177 for its fiberglass kit, and the expanded polystyrene kits publish R-values per panel or per inch, sometimes at two temperatures. Reflective kits are a different story. Some publish no R-value, and others quote figures that cannot be squared with a thin layer of foam and foil. We include the best of both types, because reflective kits do solve a real summer problem, but we are clear in each product card about what the published numbers can and cannot tell you.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also weighed fit, ease of installation and the weight a kit adds to the door, because a kit that does not fit the door's panels leaves gaps, and a heavy kit can upset the door's spring balance. Owner reports helped here: the recurring complaints are tape that lets go, panels that do not suit a particular door's pan size, and finished looks that are less tidy than the box suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these kits, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. Every R-value, dimension and coverage figure is attributed to the maker or retailer that publishes it, and where our arithmetic converts a per-inch R-value to a per-panel estimate, we say so.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="will-it-help" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much an Insulation Kit Actually Helps
          </h2>

          <p>
            A single-skin steel door has almost no insulating value of its own, so adding even a modest R-value makes a large relative difference to heat flow through the door itself. That is where claims like Owens Corning's five times better thermal performance come from. What a kit cannot do is turn a garage into a heated room. Most garages also lose heat through uninsulated walls, a concrete slab, gaps around the door and, often, an uninsulated ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an unheated garage attached to the house, the practical gains are a garage that stays a few degrees warmer in winter and cooler in summer, less cold radiating through the shared wall, and a door that is quieter and slightly more rigid. Expect a noticeable improvement, not a transformation, and do not expect your heating bills to fall much unless the garage is heated or shares a lot of wall with living space.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a heated garage or workshop, a kit makes more of a difference because you are paying to keep that space warm. Combined with sealing the door's perimeter and bottom, insulation reduces how hard a <a href="/best-garage-heater/">garage heater</a> has to work and how long it takes to warm the space. In a heated garage, choose the highest R-value kit that fits your door.
          </p>

          <p>
            In hot climates, the biggest problem is often radiant heat. A steel door in direct afternoon sun gets very hot and radiates that heat into the garage. Reflective kits are designed for exactly this, and they can make a sun-facing garage noticeably more bearable even without a high R-value.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

