        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fiberglass, Foam or Foil
          </h2>

          <p>
            Faced fiberglass kits, like Owens Corning's, use batts with a vinyl facing that tuck into the door's panel recesses and are held with clips. They offer the highest published R-value of the common kits, at R-8, and adapt to doors whose pans are slightly irregular. Fiberglass should stay dry, and the finished look is softer and less crisp than rigid panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expanded polystyrene kits, like Matador, Cellofoam and Insulfoam, use rigid panels about 1-1/4 inches thick with a hard white facing. They publish R-values of roughly 4.8 to 5.2 per panel, about half the fiberglass kit's figure, but they give a cleaner finish, resist moisture better and often install by friction-fit alone. EPS is combustible, and spec sheets such as Cellofoam's warn against leaving it exposed where a code requires protection, so check local requirements, particularly in an attached garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reflective kits use foil-faced bubble wrap or thin foam with a reflective surface. Their strength is reflecting radiant heat, which makes them effective against a sun-baked door. Their weakness is conductive heat loss in winter, which depends on thickness. Be skeptical of high R-values quoted for thin reflective products; reflective R-values are typically calculated for an assembly with an enclosed air gap and a particular direction of heat flow, which a garage door does not always provide.
          </p>

          <p>
            A rough rule: in cold climates or heated garages, choose fiberglass for maximum R-value or EPS for the best finish. In hot climates with a sun-facing door, a reflective kit is a reasonable, cheaper choice. Where both summer heat and winter cold matter, a panel kit still does more year-round.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Measuring Your Door
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the door's overall width and height. Most single doors are 8 or 9 feet wide and 7 feet tall, but 8-foot-tall doors are common on newer homes and on garages built for taller vehicles. Kits are sized for a door, not by square footage alone: Owens Corning's covers a single door up to 9 feet by 7 feet 4 inches, while the standard Matador kit is sized for 7-foot doors. A double door usually needs two single-door kits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then measure the panel recesses, sometimes called pans, on the inside of the door. Each horizontal section of a steel door is divided into rectangles by vertical stiles. Panel kits are cut to fit these openings, typically around 20 to 22 inches tall and up to 54 inches wide. Measure several, because they are not always identical, and compare them with the kit's panel size before you buy. Rigid foam is less forgiving than fiberglass if your pans are larger or irregular.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the door's depth too. The stiles and the door's hardware set how thick the insulation can be before it fouls rollers, hinges or the opener's track. Most kits are 1-1/4 to 2-1/4 inches thick, which suits most steel doors, but check clearance at the hinges before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, note what the door is made of. These kits are designed for single-skin steel doors with exposed recesses. Wood doors, aluminum-and-glass doors and doors that are already insulated or have a steel back skin are not good candidates, as we explain at the end of this guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installing a Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Close the door, clean the inside face of each panel and let it dry. Most kits go in with the door down, working section by section. Rigid foam panels are measured, scored and snapped to size with a utility knife and straightedge, then pushed into the pan so the edges are held by the stiles. Fiberglass batts are cut to length and secured with the retainer clips supplied, which typically attach to the door skin with adhesive pads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reflective kits are measured and cut from rolls, then attached with double-sided tape. Tape adhesion is the most common complaint with these kits, especially in very hot or very cold garages, so clean and degrease the steel thoroughly and apply the tape at a moderate temperature. Some owners add a bead of construction adhesive or extra tape at the corners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work around the hardware. Cut neatly around hinges, roller brackets and the opener's arm bracket, and never cover anything that moves or that you may need to adjust later. Leave the door's warning labels visible where possible, and keep insulation clear of the spring assembly and cables.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect an afternoon for a single door. The job is mostly measuring and cutting, and it is well within reach of most homeowners. The one part that is not a DIY task is spring adjustment, which we cover next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Weight, Balance and the Springs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every garage door is balanced by its springs, so that the door can be lifted by hand and the opener only provides a little force. Adding insulation adds weight. Light reflective kits add very little, and rigid foam is light too, but any added weight shifts the balance slightly. In most cases the change is small, but it is worth checking.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check balance, close the door, pull the opener's emergency release so the door is disconnected, and lift it by hand to about halfway. A balanced door stays roughly where you leave it. If it drops quickly or shoots up, the springs need adjusting. Reconnect the opener afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not adjust torsion springs yourself. They are under very high tension and can cause serious injury if handled without the right tools and training. If the door is out of balance after insulating, call a garage door technician. A properly balanced door also protects the opener; an unbalanced door makes the opener's motor and gears work harder, which shortens its life. If yours is struggling already, our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-door-opener/">best garage door openers</a> covers replacements, and our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-garage-door-openers-last/">how long garage door openers last</a> covers the signs of wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some opener manufacturers also recommend resetting the opener's force and travel limits after any change to the door. Check your opener's manual and run its safety reversal test after installing a kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Seal the Gaps Too
          </h2>

          <p>
            Insulating the door panels without sealing the door's edges is like wearing a coat with the zipper open. Most of the cold that gets into a garage in winter arrives as air leaking under the door and around its sides and top, not by conduction through the steel. If you can see daylight around a closed door, that gap matters more than the door's R-value.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bottom seal is usually the worst offender. Rubber bottom seals harden, crack and get torn, and an uneven slab leaves gaps the seal cannot reach. Replacing it is cheap and easy on most doors; our guide to the <a href="/best-garage-door-bottom-seal/">best garage door bottom seals</a> covers the retainer types and the threshold seals that fix uneven floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check the perimeter weatherstrip on the door jambs and header. This vinyl or rubber strip should press lightly against the door's face when it is closed. If it is split, flattened or missing, replace it. Adjust it so it seals without dragging on the door, which would make the opener work harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look between the door sections. Some steel doors have weatherstrip in the joints, and some do not. Gaps between sections leak air and can whistle in wind. Insulation panels cover the steel, not the joints, so seal the gaps separately if they are large.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cost and Payback
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garage door insulation kits are among the cheaper home insulation projects. Single-door kits are typically priced somewhere around one to two hundred dollars; one retailer listed the Matador kit at about $145. Reflective kits are often cheaper, and double doors usually need two single-door kits or a two-car reflective kit. Add a new bottom seal and perimeter weatherstrip and the whole project usually stays within a few hundred dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Payback depends on whether the garage is heated. In an unheated, detached garage there may be no energy saving at all, and the benefit is comfort while you work out there. In an attached garage, the benefit is a warmer shared wall and a slightly warmer room above, which can be noticeable but rarely shows up dramatically on a bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a heated garage or workshop, the payback is real, because you are paying to keep the space warm and the door is often the largest uninsulated surface. Combined with air sealing, a kit can noticeably reduce how long a heater runs. In a cooled garage in a hot climate, a reflective kit on a sun-facing door can make a similar difference to how hard an air conditioner or fan works.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare all of this with replacing the door. An insulated steel door with a foam core and a steel back skin performs better and looks finished on both sides, but costs several times as much as a kit plus installation. If the door is old, damaged or noisy anyway, putting the money toward a new insulated door can make more sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip a Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip a kit if your door is already insulated. Doors with a polystyrene or polyurethane core and a steel back skin have no open recesses to fill, and adding a layer to the back skin adds weight with little benefit. Check the inside of the door: if it is a smooth steel sheet rather than ribbed pans, it is probably already insulated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip a kit, or choose carefully, if your door is wood, aluminum and glass, or a lightweight design that the springs barely hold. Wood doors need to breathe and can trap moisture behind insulation, glass panels cannot be insulated with a kit, and a door whose springs are already marginal may be pushed out of balance by any added weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, too, if your goal is to heat the garage and the walls and ceiling are bare. Insulating the door alone helps, but the walls, the ceiling and air sealing usually make a bigger combined difference. Start with the largest uninsulated surfaces and the obvious air leaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else with a bare steel door, a kit is one of the most cost-effective upgrades you can make to a garage. Choose a panel kit with a published R-value for cold weather, or a reflective kit for summer sun, seal the gaps around the door, and check the door's balance when you are done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Temperature Claim Over an R-Value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Claims that a kit lowers garage temperature by 20 or 40 degrees depend on conditions you cannot check. Compare kits on their published R-value and the test standard behind it. If a kit publishes no R-value, or an implausibly high one for its thickness, treat its claims with caution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Gaps Around the Door</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most winter cold enters a garage as air leaking under and around the door, not through the steel. Replace a worn bottom seal and perimeter weatherstrip at the same time, or the insulation will make far less difference than you expect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Wrong Size for the Door</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kits are sized for a door, not just by area. A standard kit for a 7-foot door will not cover an 8-foot door, and rigid panels must match your door's pan size. Measure the door and several pans before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adjusting the Springs Yourself</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Added insulation can affect the door's balance. Check it with the opener disconnected, but if it needs adjustment, call a technician. Torsion springs are under extreme tension and can cause serious injury.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering Hardware or Labels</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Insulation should never cover hinges, rollers, cables, springs or the opener's bracket. Cut neatly around anything that moves or needs adjusting, and keep the door's warning labels visible.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best garage door insulation kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an uninsulated single steel door, the Owens Corning kit is the best all-round choice because Owens Corning publishes R-8 under ASTM C177, the highest figure among the panel kits we found. If you prefer rigid foam panels with a cleaner finish, the Matador, Cellofoam and Insulfoam kits publish roughly R-4.8 to R-5.2 per panel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is insulating a garage door worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes for attached or heated garages, and often for comfort in unheated ones. A bare steel door has almost no insulating value, so even a modest kit noticeably reduces heat flow through the door. Pair it with new seals around the door for the biggest difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What R-value do I need for a garage door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single requirement, but higher is better in cold climates and heated garages. Among common kits, faced fiberglass at R-8 is the highest published figure we found, and EPS panel kits are around R-5. Reflective kits often publish no R-value or quote figures that depend on an air gap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do reflective garage door insulation kits work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work best against radiant heat, such as a steel door baking in afternoon sun, and can make a hot garage noticeably more comfortable. They are much less effective against winter heat loss. Be skeptical of high R-values quoted for thin reflective products.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will insulation make my garage door too heavy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most kits add only a few pounds, and reflective kits add very little. It can still shift the door's balance. Disconnect the opener and lift the door halfway; if it does not stay put, have a technician adjust the springs rather than doing it yourself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I insulate a double garage door?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Most single-door panel kits are used two at a time on a 16- or 18-foot double door, and some reflective kits are sold in two-car sizes. Measure the door and the pans, since double doors often have wider pans than single doors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you insulate a wood or glass garage door with a kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                These kits are designed for single-skin steel doors with open pans. Wood doors can trap moisture behind insulation, and glass panels cannot be insulated with a kit. If your door is wood or largely glass, a replacement insulated door is usually the better route.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

